WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:21.300
Socrates is considered the father of Western
philosophy.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.699
He was a man who questioned everything and
everyone.

00:00:24.699 --> 00:00:29.340
With no regard for how his opinions would
be received, he imposed his views with a dogmatic

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:33.120
superiority that sealed his eventual fate.

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:38.579
Sill, his great legacy to the world was the
method of questioning to arrive at the truth

00:00:38.579 --> 00:00:40.280
known as the Socratic method.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:46.790
Today on Biographics, we discover the man
who was Socrates.

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:52.680
Beginnings
Of all the great figures of antiquity, none

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:55.570
are as frustrating for the historian as Socrates.

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:59.409
The man never wrote down anything that has
survived, so the only records of his life

00:00:59.409 --> 00:01:01.330
come from secondary sources.

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:07.549
The two main ones are from Plato, his devoted
student, and Xenophon, a contemporary of Plato.

00:01:07.549 --> 00:01:11.759
These writings mainly consist of records of
conversations that often included Socrates

00:01:11.759 --> 00:01:12.759
himself.

00:01:12.759 --> 00:01:16.330
Discerning the facts of his life from such
writings is a challenge.

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:21.190
In fact, this lack of solid biographical information
is known as the Socratic problem.

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:27.930
Socrates was either born in 469 or 470 B.C.E.
in a Greek village located a twenty-minute

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:29.420
walk away from Athens.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:33.720
His father, Sophroniscus, was a sculptor while
his mother, Phaenarete, worked as a mid-wife.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:38.580
Sophroniscus wanted his son to take on the
craft of a sculptor so, during his youth,

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:40.290
he was apprenticed to his father.

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:43.780
He apparently became an accomplished sculptor
with tradition having it that he worked on

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:48.360
a well-known statue entitled the Three Muses
in Their Habits, which was on display at the

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:49.360
Acropolis.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:53.320
As a young man, Socrates, devoured the writings
of the Greek philosopher Anaxagoras.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:57.380
It is believed that he may have actually studied
under this master but that cannot be determined

00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:58.380
with certainty.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:02.890
The basic teaching of Anaxagoras were that
all things have always existed, but in their

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:06.180
original form were in tiny fragments of their
current form.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:11.140
In Athens, the Agora, or public square, was
the central meeting place for men to discuss

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:13.340
politics, art and philosophy.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:16.950
As he was still a youth, Socrates was not
eligible to attend the Agora.

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:20.150
Instead he spent much of his free time in
the workshops of the city’s merchants where

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:22.670
he would engage them in philosophical questions.

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:26.450
After some time working as a sculptor, Socrates
went off to study under the philosophical

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:28.010
master Archelaus.

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:32.630
He studied mathematics and astronomy and became
familiar with the work of earlier philosophers.

00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:37.290
According to a 3rd Century biographer by the
name of DiogenesLaertius, Archelaus embarked

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:42.160
upon a homosexual relationship with Socrates,
a practice which was widespread in ancient

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:43.160
Greece.

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:46.510
At the time that Socrates began showing an
interest in philosophy, the discipline was

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:47.870
about a hundred years old.

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:52.540
The ideas that were being thrown around were
largely theoretical, bearing little practical

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:53.540
application.

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:57.100
Socrates soon came to the realization that
the things that he was learning didn’t have

00:02:57.100 --> 00:02:58.870
any benefit to society.

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:02.880
He was far more interested in addressing the
problems of everyday life.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:07.050
Despite his curiosity to understand the world
he was not a scientific thinker.

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:11.130
Rather than questioning the world to understand
how it worked, he believed that we would be

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:15.520
better off questioning ourselves in order
to understand how we work.

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:22.250
He adopted the quote which has often been
wrongly attributed to him – Know Thyself.

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:27.850
The Soldier-Philosopher
With the outbreak of the Peloponnesian War

00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:34.100
in 431 B.C.E, Socrates was recruited as a
hoplite, the equivalent of a private, third

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:35.100
class.

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:37.450
He immediately stood out among his fellow
conscripts.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:41.290
Even though there is much discrepancy about
the details of his life, there is one thing

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:46.069
about Socrates upon which there is universal
agreement – he was ugly.

00:03:46.069 --> 00:03:49.129
He had a snub nose and his hair was long and
unkempt.

00:03:49.129 --> 00:03:53.590
He also showed no interest in personal grooming
and rarely bathed.

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:57.120
We know about Socrates’ military service
from the writings of Plato.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:01.070
In Plato’s work, Apology, he tells us that
his master fought in the battles of Amphipolis,

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:02.739
Delium and Potidaea.

00:04:02.739 --> 00:04:07.450
Another chronicler, Alcibiades, specifically
mentions Socrates’ heroic actions in the

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:11.510
Battle of Delium, stating that the philosopher
saved his life during the fighting.

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:15.840
Having completed his military service, in
406 B.C.E., Socrates was appointed as a member

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:20.560
of the boule, which was a 500-member council
which ran the daily affairs of Athens.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:24.840
Xenophon tells us that he was the overseer
of a debate held to determine the fates of

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:29.340
a number of military commanders who had abandoned
their wounded in battle in order to pursue

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:30.340
the Spartans.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:33.870
The public were clamoring for the men to be
put to death, but Socrates blocked the vote

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:38.520
and the men’s lives were spared.

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:43.690
Challenging Society
With the end of the Peloponnesian war in 404

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:48.181
BC, there was a period of upheaval in Athens
that resulted in the coming to power of a

00:04:48.181 --> 00:04:50.740
group known as the Thirty Tyrants.

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:55.020
These men instituted a period of terror marked
by bloodshed and confiscation of property.

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:59.590
After eight months, the rebel regime fell
and was replaced with a democratic Greek government,

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:02.840
but that hardly meant that life was going
to get better for Socrates.

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:05.770
In fact, it was about to get a whole lot worse.

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:08.229
Socrates was an opponent of democracy.

00:05:08.229 --> 00:05:12.560
He believed that the people as a whole were
incapable of self-rule.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:15.810
They needed a wise shepherd to guide them
in life.

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:19.900
He further claimed that the ordinary citizen
does not possess any innate virtue that would

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:23.020
give him the ability to speak on behalf of
others.

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:27.610
Virtue, he maintained, was something that
is attained only by knowledge and that knowledge

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:30.610
was beyond the scope of the everyday person.

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:35.470
These ideas were sharply at odds with those
of the Athenian society in which Socrates

00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:36.470
lived.

00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:41.120
The people cherished the enlightened right
of every citizen to have a voice in the assembly.

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:47.320
As a result, the ideas of Socrates were viewed
with suspicion, if not outright hostility.

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:51.710
Things weren’t helped by the rather condescending
and arrogant manner in which he expressed

00:05:51.710 --> 00:05:52.710
his views.

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:56.090
He was dismissive of those who held contrary
views to his own.

00:05:56.090 --> 00:05:58.690
This frequently led to violent reactions.

00:05:58.690 --> 00:06:02.180
Diogenes records that . . .
"men set upon him with their fists or tore

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:03.720
his hair out,".

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:07.710
Faced with this, Socrates "bore all this ill-usage
patiently."

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:12.460
A fascinating contemporary view of Socrates
is provided by the Athenian comic playwright

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:13.460
Aristophanes.

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:16.820
In 423 B.C.E, he wrote a play called The Clouds.

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:20.340
In that play he has a character who is clearly
based on Socrates.

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:25.760
This character, the headmaster of a ‘thinkery’,
is presented as a comic buffoon who ‘stalks

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:30.419
the streets’ barefoot, looking down his
nose at the ignorant masses and ‘rolling

00:06:30.419 --> 00:06:34.250
his eyes’ at the unintelligible guttural
that he hears.

00:06:34.250 --> 00:06:38.500
Socrates was apparently friendly with Aristophanes
and held no grudge against such portrayals.

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:41.100
In fact, Plutarch quotes him as saying the
following . . .

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:47.150
When they break a jest upon me in the theatre,
I feel as if I were at a big party of good

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:48.150
friends.

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:52.520
It appears, then, that, around the 420’s
Socrates was seen by most people as a local

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:56.130
oddball, who was slightly annoying but generally
harmless.

00:06:56.130 --> 00:06:58.990
But that view was to change.

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:04.199
The democracy that Athenians so cherished
was challenged on two occasions during Socrates’

00:07:04.199 --> 00:07:05.380
later years.

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:08.970
During a four-month period in 411-410 B.C.E.

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:11.050
Athenian democracy was overthrown.

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:17.580
Then in 404 B.C.E the Thirty Tyrants usurped
democracy, ushering in eight months of chaos.

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:22.310
Both of these insurrections were led by former
students of Socrates, Alcibiades and Critias,

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:23.680
relatives of Plato.

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:28.160
The first of these insurrections turned public
opinion against Socrates as the brains behind

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:29.160
the coup.

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:33.720
With the bloodshed caused by the second overthrow,
people began to indirectly blame the philosopher

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:34.730
for the carnage.

00:07:34.730 --> 00:07:39.900
With the coming to power of the Thirty Tyrants,
the former leaders of the democracy fled to

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:40.900
the mountains.

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:41.900
There they planned their resistance.

00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:45.990
The elders of the new totalitarian government
did not directly involve Socrates in the carrying

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:50.410
out of their wicked acts – except on one
occasion.

00:07:50.410 --> 00:07:54.880
They summoned him along with four other men
and ordered them to go to Salamis and apprehend

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:59.539
a learned man of influence by the name of
Leon and then bring him back to Athens to

00:07:59.539 --> 00:08:00.610
be executed.

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:04.940
This man was an upstanding, honest citizen
who had done nothing deserving of condemnation,

00:08:04.940 --> 00:08:06.430
let alone death.

00:08:06.430 --> 00:08:11.349
Socrates refused to do this dirty work, stating
that he would never perform an act that compromised

00:08:11.349 --> 00:08:12.349
his integrity.

00:08:12.349 --> 00:08:15.830
However, he did nothing to warn Leon of the
impending danger.

00:08:15.830 --> 00:08:20.050
As a result, the other four men went ahead
and apprehended Leon and he was summarily

00:08:20.050 --> 00:08:21.130
put to death.

00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:25.970
This failure to pro-actively warn Leon would
come back to haunt Socrates at his upcoming

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:26.970
trial.

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:31.080
Rather than being viewed as the local eccentric,
Socrates was now being seen by many as a dangerous

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:35.519
spreader of radical ideas that were leading
others to the most vile excesses.

00:08:35.519 --> 00:08:41.060
By 403 B.C.E the rule of the Thirty Tyrants
was over and the former democratic government

00:08:41.060 --> 00:08:42.060
had been restored.

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:46.480
A general amnesty had been enacted which meant
that no-one, including Socrates, could be

00:08:46.480 --> 00:09:13.339
brought up on charges for their actions during
the eight months

00:09:13.339 --> 00:09:41.800
of tyranny.

00:09:41.800 --> 00:10:07.439
Trial

00:10:07.439 --> 00:10:11.089
Socrates was finally arrested in 399 BCE.

00:10:11.089 --> 00:10:15.079
By then the state had four years since the
end of the last revolt to come up with some

00:10:15.079 --> 00:10:16.389
charges against him.

00:10:16.389 --> 00:10:21.299
During that period Socrates appeared to be
undeterred by the return to favor of democracy

00:10:21.299 --> 00:10:25.660
and the antipathy towards those who espoused
views in opposition to it.

00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:29.730
He continued to deride democracy and to champion
rule by a strong leader.

00:10:29.730 --> 00:10:34.589
When there was another attempted overthrow
in 401, the state had had enough of his revolutionizing

00:10:34.589 --> 00:10:36.290
of impressionable youth.

00:10:36.290 --> 00:10:40.430
It was now just a matter of time before he
was brought to account.

00:10:40.430 --> 00:10:44.339
Athenian law allowed for any citizen to bring
criminal proceedings against another.

00:10:44.339 --> 00:10:47.970
A man named Meletus brought an oral summons
against Socrates.

00:10:47.970 --> 00:10:52.689
The summons required him to appear before
a legal magistrate to face charges of impiety

00:10:52.689 --> 00:10:54.259
and of corrupting youth.

00:10:54.259 --> 00:10:58.699
The magistrate heard out the applicant and
determined that there were grounds for a preliminary

00:10:58.699 --> 00:10:59.699
hearing.

00:10:59.699 --> 00:11:03.019
At that hearing the charges were read out
and Socrates was given the opportunity to

00:11:03.019 --> 00:11:04.019
answer them.

00:11:04.019 --> 00:11:07.680
A document containing the charges was then
drawn up, long with the requested penalty.

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:10.779
It read . . .
Socrates is guilty of refusing to recognize

00:11:10.779 --> 00:11:14.569
the gods recognized by the state, and of introducing
new divinities.

00:11:14.569 --> 00:11:17.720
He is also guilty of corrupting the youth.

00:11:17.720 --> 00:11:20.290
The penalty demanded is death.

00:11:20.290 --> 00:11:24.779
The actual trial took place over a period
of nine hours in the Agora in the center of

00:11:24.779 --> 00:11:25.779
Athens.

00:11:25.779 --> 00:11:28.889
The jury was made up of 500 citizens, drawn
by lot.

00:11:28.889 --> 00:11:33.089
The large hury number was standard in Ancient
trials and seems to have been mainly to prevent

00:11:33.089 --> 00:11:35.399
bribery from swaying the outcome of cases.

00:11:35.399 --> 00:11:39.689
After the reading of the charges, the three
main accusers of Socrates were given three

00:11:39.689 --> 00:11:41.329
hours to present their case.

00:11:41.329 --> 00:11:43.839
There is no surviving record of their testimony.

00:11:43.839 --> 00:11:49.029
We do know, however, that the main accuser
was not Meletus, but a certain Anytus, whom

00:11:49.029 --> 00:11:52.709
was a politician who harbored ongoing resentments
against Socrates.

00:11:52.709 --> 00:11:57.739
It is believed that the son of Antyus was
a student with whom Socrates had a homosexual

00:11:57.739 --> 00:12:01.129
relationship, something which greatly upset
the father.

00:12:01.129 --> 00:12:05.239
It is likely that the main thrust of the accusations
was that Socrates was leading young people

00:12:05.239 --> 00:12:09.050
down a dangerous political path with his anti-democratic
teachings.

00:12:09.050 --> 00:12:13.829
However, in Plato’s Apology, in which he
provides an account of Socrates’ defense,

00:12:13.829 --> 00:12:17.269
considerable weight is given to his defense
of the charge of impiety.

00:12:17.269 --> 00:12:21.720
Piety in those days involved not only respect
for the Greek Gods, but also for the dead

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:23.269
and for one’s ancestors.

00:12:23.269 --> 00:12:28.000
If there was even one impious person in the
city, it was a serious matter, as that one’s

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:31.769
incursions might bring the wrath of the Gods
upon the entire city.

00:12:31.769 --> 00:12:37.079
Even though the Athenians had no written scriptural
code and no priesthood, there were a significant

00:12:37.079 --> 00:12:41.249
number of rituals, observances and offerings
that were required.

00:12:41.249 --> 00:12:45.369
It is highly likely that Socrates neglected
to observe the rituals of the Gods that would

00:12:45.369 --> 00:12:46.989
have been expected of him.

00:12:46.989 --> 00:12:50.120
Furthermore, he would have argued that the
generally accepted view of worship as being

00:12:50.120 --> 00:12:52.290
based on rituals was wrong.

00:12:52.290 --> 00:12:55.959
According to Xenophon, Socrates claimed to
have the ability to communicate directly with

00:12:55.959 --> 00:12:56.959
the gods.

00:12:56.959 --> 00:13:01.309
This would have infuriated those in attendance
at his trial, including the 500 jurors.

00:13:01.309 --> 00:13:05.980
It is difficult for us to know just what,
if any, religious beliefs, Socrates actually

00:13:05.980 --> 00:13:06.980
held.

00:13:06.980 --> 00:13:11.800
Throughout his dialogues, Plato has him referring
to both one God and to gods.

00:13:11.800 --> 00:13:15.709
It has also been suggested that he viewed
the word God as referring to the philosophical

00:13:15.709 --> 00:13:17.339
idea of goodness.

00:13:17.339 --> 00:13:21.720
A critical part of the accusations against
him had to do with his close association with

00:13:21.720 --> 00:13:26.189
the leaders of the Thirty Tyrants rebellion,
specifically his ties to the leader of the

00:13:26.189 --> 00:13:27.470
revolt, Critias.

00:13:27.470 --> 00:13:30.999
In his defense, Socrates brought up the time
when he was ordered to go and arrest Leon

00:13:30.999 --> 00:13:31.999
of Salamis.

00:13:31.999 --> 00:13:36.339
He claimed that his refusal to do so was a
clear indication that he was not in cahoots

00:13:36.339 --> 00:13:40.079
with the rebels and was, instead, a loyal
citizen of Athens.

00:13:40.079 --> 00:13:44.929
But his accuser countered that a loyal citizen
would have done more than simply refrain from

00:13:44.929 --> 00:13:49.920
the bad action; he would have warned the innocent
man and, therefore, saved his life.

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:52.480
Socrates spoke in his own defense for three
full hours.

00:13:52.480 --> 00:13:56.959
Of the dozens of versions of it that were
made, only two have survived down to our time

00:13:56.959 --> 00:13:59.579
– those of Plato and Xenophon.

00:13:59.579 --> 00:14:04.419
The versions agree that the master was defiant
in his tone, almost as if he were inviting

00:14:04.419 --> 00:14:05.529
an adverse judgement.

00:14:05.529 --> 00:14:09.459
As part of his defense, Socrates told the
jurors that he was a hero.

00:14:09.459 --> 00:14:12.100
He recalled his exemplary military service.

00:14:12.100 --> 00:14:16.610
He then extended the thought with an analogy,
telling them he had fought for decades to

00:14:16.610 --> 00:14:18.739
save the souls of young Athenians.

00:14:18.739 --> 00:14:24.319
If teaching virtue made him guilty of corruption,
he said then was guilty as charged.

00:14:24.319 --> 00:14:26.540
He then assured his listeners with the following
. . .

00:14:26.540 --> 00:14:31.900
Men of Athens, I honor and love you; but I
shall obey God rather than you, and while

00:14:31.900 --> 00:14:37.889
I have life and strength I shall never cease
from the practice and teaching of philosophy.

00:14:37.889 --> 00:14:41.709
In the face of the charge that he was a corrupter
of the youth of Athens, Socrates stated that

00:14:41.709 --> 00:14:43.549
he never claimed to be a teacher.

00:14:43.549 --> 00:14:47.549
He was merely a man who happened to spend
time strolling around the city.

00:14:47.549 --> 00:14:51.579
If he was asked a question, he would answer
it to the best of his ability.

00:14:51.579 --> 00:14:55.230
Then, pointing to Plato and others of his
followers in the crowd he pointed out that

00:14:55.230 --> 00:14:57.749
none of them had ever laid an accusation against
him.

00:14:57.749 --> 00:15:01.800
Those of his former students who had done
bad, he said, did so because they corrupted

00:15:01.800 --> 00:15:03.549
the things that he had said.

00:15:03.549 --> 00:15:07.929
As the marathon speech drew to a close it
was obvious to everyone listening that Socrates

00:15:07.929 --> 00:15:12.319
had no intention of stopping doing whatever
it was that people objected to.

00:15:12.319 --> 00:15:14.910
The only way to stop him was to put him to
death.

00:15:14.910 --> 00:15:19.360
Contrary to normal practice in such proceedings,
he made no pleas for mercy.

00:15:19.360 --> 00:15:24.009
After nine hours of proceedings, the jury
of 500 were tasked with, making their decision.

00:15:24.009 --> 00:15:26.839
Unlike today, the jury did not retire to discuss
their thoughts.

00:15:26.839 --> 00:15:32.370
They simply voted by placing a disk into one
of two urns, labelled guilty or not guilty,

00:15:32.370 --> 00:15:35.139
with the urn that had held the most votes
determining the outcome.

00:15:35.139 --> 00:15:40.309
Once they had all completed this task, there
were 280 disks in the guilty urn and 220 in

00:15:40.309 --> 00:15:41.389
the not guilty one.

00:15:41.389 --> 00:15:42.970
The jury’s job was not yet over.

00:15:42.970 --> 00:15:44.709
Now they had to determine the penalty.

00:15:44.709 --> 00:15:49.609
They accusers of Socrates rose and put forward
their case that the condemned impious corrupter

00:15:49.609 --> 00:15:52.109
of the youth of Athens must be put to death.

00:15:52.109 --> 00:15:55.680
Socrates himself was then invited to put forward
his case for leniency.

00:15:55.680 --> 00:15:59.829
This was his opportunity to win some favor
with the jury, perhaps by suggesting that

00:15:59.829 --> 00:16:00.920
he be sent into exile.

00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:06.319
But, true to his nature, he refused to step
down from his position of superior indignation.

00:16:06.319 --> 00:16:10.709
He should, he suggested, be rewarded rather
than condemned for his actions.

00:16:10.709 --> 00:16:15.369
The court officials were not impressed and
he was told to give a more realistic alternative

00:16:15.369 --> 00:16:16.369
penalty.

00:16:16.369 --> 00:16:19.639
Socrates then offered that he could be subjected
to a modest fine.

00:16:19.639 --> 00:16:25.019
Faced with these two extremes of punishment,
the jurors chose death.

00:16:25.019 --> 00:16:31.799
The Death of Socrates
With the trial now over, Socrates was taken

00:16:31.799 --> 00:16:33.359
to an adjoining prison cell.

00:16:33.359 --> 00:16:38.129
According to the record of his final days
penned by Plato, Socrates was given the opportunity

00:16:38.129 --> 00:16:42.049
to escape when he was visited in his cell
by his wealthy friend Crito.

00:16:42.049 --> 00:16:45.359
This man had already laid plans to smuggle
Socrates out of Athens.

00:16:45.359 --> 00:16:49.439
However, the condemned man showed no interest
in escaping his fate.

00:16:49.439 --> 00:16:53.759
The conversation between the two men, as Crito
attempted to persuade Socrates to escape,

00:16:53.759 --> 00:16:59.829
and the great philosopher’s responses present
an important aspect of Socrates’ moral code.

00:16:59.829 --> 00:17:04.579
Crito put forward three points as to why Socrates
should escape from his death sentence.

00:17:04.579 --> 00:17:08.709
Firstly, the death would be a bad reflection
on the followers of Socrates, as people would

00:17:08.709 --> 00:17:10.620
think that they abandoned him.

00:17:10.620 --> 00:17:14.680
Secondly, all finances involved in the escape
were covered and, furthermore, a comfortable

00:17:14.680 --> 00:17:16.460
life in exile had been arranged.

00:17:16.460 --> 00:17:19.839
Finally, his death would leave his sons without
a father.

00:17:19.839 --> 00:17:23.610
In response, Socrates countered that a man
should not worry about public opinion, but

00:17:23.610 --> 00:17:25.040
only be guided wisdom.

00:17:25.040 --> 00:17:29.299
The only real question to consider in the
situation was where or not it was just for

00:17:29.299 --> 00:17:32.280
Socrates to escape his condemnation to death.

00:17:32.280 --> 00:17:36.580
He then spoke of the Laws of Athens, stating
that to break one of those laws meant breaking

00:17:36.580 --> 00:17:37.580
all of them.

00:17:37.580 --> 00:17:41.140
To go against the laws of Athens would be
like a child rebelling against a parent, he

00:17:41.140 --> 00:17:42.140
argued.

00:17:42.140 --> 00:17:44.530
If he were to dare to go against the laws
of the city, he asserted, he would receive

00:17:44.530 --> 00:17:46.950
a harsh judgment in the underworld.

00:17:46.950 --> 00:17:49.750
As a result, Socrates refused the offer of
escape.

00:17:49.750 --> 00:17:51.649
His fate was now sealed.

00:17:51.649 --> 00:17:55.700
Plato chronicles his death in his work . In
it he gives an eyewitness account from the

00:17:55.700 --> 00:17:58.100
viewpoint of fictional character Phaedo.

00:17:58.100 --> 00:18:02.179
According to the account, a man came into
the cell carrying a cup of poisoned hemlock.

00:18:02.179 --> 00:18:06.380
Socrates turned to him and said . . .
Now good sir, you understand these things.

00:18:06.380 --> 00:18:07.750
Tell me what I must do.

00:18:07.750 --> 00:18:11.500
The man replied . . .
Just drink it and walk around until your legs

00:18:11.500 --> 00:18:13.770
begin to feel heavy, then lie down.

00:18:13.770 --> 00:18:15.409
It will soon act.

00:18:15.409 --> 00:18:16.710
The cup was then offered to Socrates.

00:18:16.710 --> 00:18:21.200
He took it happily, and then asked if it was
permissible for him to offer a pledge before

00:18:21.200 --> 00:18:22.200
drinking it.

00:18:22.200 --> 00:18:25.210
Permission was granted, and he offered the
following prayer . . .;

00:18:25.210 --> 00:18:29.700
we can and must pray to the gods that our
sojourn on earth will continue happy beyond

00:18:29.700 --> 00:18:30.700
the grave.

00:18:30.700 --> 00:18:33.950
This is my prayer, and may it come to pass.

00:18:33.950 --> 00:18:36.960
At this he drank the poison without hesitation.

00:18:36.960 --> 00:18:40.750
Those of his friends who were in the cell
with him gave way to tears, prompting Socrates

00:18:40.750 --> 00:18:44.429
to declare . . .
You are strange fellows; what is wrong with

00:18:44.429 --> 00:18:45.429
you?

00:18:45.429 --> 00:18:48.580
I sent the women away for this very purpose,
to stop their creating such a scene.

00:18:48.580 --> 00:18:51.299
I have heard that one should die in silence.

00:18:51.299 --> 00:18:54.710
So please be quiet and keep control of yourselves.

00:18:54.710 --> 00:18:56.799
For the next few minutes he walked around
the room.

00:18:56.799 --> 00:19:01.880
Then, when his legs became heavy, he lay on
his bed with a covering over his face.

00:19:01.880 --> 00:19:05.720
Slowly he lost feeling, first in his legs
and then moving up his body.

00:19:05.720 --> 00:19:11.010
His last words, directed to Crito, were . . .
Crito, we owe a cock to Asclepius.

00:19:11.010 --> 00:19:12.010
Do pay it.

00:19:12.010 --> 00:19:13.130
Don't forget.

00:19:13.130 --> 00:19:14.659
This seems a rather strange request.

00:19:14.659 --> 00:19:18.460
But it makes sense when we realize that Asclepius
was the God of Good Health.

00:19:18.460 --> 00:19:23.049
The sacrifice of a cock was a normal offering
of thanks for recovery from illness.

00:19:23.049 --> 00:19:28.610
Socrates believed that he was cured of the
disease of life and was, as a result, not

00:19:28.610 --> 00:19:30.309
frightened of death.

00:19:30.309 --> 00:19:33.200
Crito then asked him if there was anything
else, he wanted to say.

00:19:33.200 --> 00:19:34.809
But there was no reply.

00:19:34.809 --> 00:19:39.140
The poison had reached his heart and the life
of Socrates was drained away.

00:19:39.140 --> 00:19:42.539
Plato concluded his account with these words
. . .

00:19:42.539 --> 00:19:48.230
This was the end of our friend, the best,
wisest and most upright man of any that I

00:19:48.230 --> 00:20:08.049
have ever known.

